# Tensor Polarizable Vacuum  
## An Exactly General-Relativistic Constitutive Interpretation of Spacetime

### Abstract

We formulate the **Tensor Polarizable Vacuum (TPV)** as a constitutive interpretation of general relativity rather than as a modified theory of gravity. The central ontological statement is that the vacuum is not a material field embedded in spacetime: the vacuum is spacetime itself. Its “polarization” is a constitutive representation of the same geometric state ordinarily represented by the Lorentzian metric. To make this statement mathematically precise, we associate with every spacetime metric \(g_{\mu\nu}\) a metric-induced constitutive state consisting of the Hodge dual acting on two-forms together with the metric volume form. The Hodge operator determines the conformal metric in four dimensions, while the volume form fixes the otherwise undetermined conformal scale. Consequently the pair provides, under fixed orientation and Lorentzian signature, an invertible representation of the metric.

We define the TPV action by pulling the Einstein-Hilbert-matter action through this constitutive map and prove that its stationary points are in one-to-one correspondence with solutions of the Einstein field equations. Diffeomorphism gauge orbits, causal structure, geodesic motion, stress-energy conservation, the Hamiltonian and momentum constraints, and the two local tensor degrees of freedom are preserved. An equivalent Einstein-Palatini representation shows that an independent affine connection remains auxiliary, reducing to the Levi-Civita connection up to projective gauge. Linear gravitational waves therefore possess only the standard \(+\) and \(\times\) polarizations, represented in TPV language as propagating tensor perturbations of the constitutive state.

The construction gives no observable departure from general relativity and is not presented as a microscopic theory of a material ether. Rather, it supplies a precise constitutive interpretation of the physical meaning assigned to spacetime geometry. Curvature is interpreted not as an additional field acting upon the vacuum but as the invariant nonuniformity and nonintegrability of the vacuum-spacetime constitutive state itself. We distinguish this exactly equivalent theory from physically active TPV extensions, in which additional constitutive response would produce genuine departures from general relativity.

