## 3. Reconstruction theorem

Consider two Lorentzian metrics \(g\) and \(\tilde g\), with the same orientation and time orientation, such that

\[
\star_g=\star_{\tilde g}
\tag{12}
\]

on all two-forms.

The constitutive-metric reconstruction results imply that the two metrics must be conformally related:

\[
\tilde g_{\mu\nu}
=
\Omega^2g_{\mu\nu}.
\tag{13}
\]

Their volume forms satisfy

\[
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\tilde g}
=
\Omega^4\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_g.
\tag{14}
\]

If the complete TPV states agree,

\[
\mathcal C[\tilde g]
=
\mathcal C[g],
\tag{15}
\]

then

\[
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\tilde g}
=
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_g,
\tag{16}
\]

and hence

\[
\Omega^4=1.
\tag{17}
\]

Taking the positive conformal factor compatible with unchanged Lorentzian signature and orientation gives

\[
\Omega=1.
\tag{18}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal C[\tilde g]=\mathcal C[g]
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\tilde g=g.
}
\tag{19}
\]

Conversely, equality of the metrics trivially gives equality of their constitutive states.

Thus the map

\[
\Phi:
g_{\mu\nu}
\longmapsto
\mathcal C[g]
\tag{20}
\]

is one-to-one onto its image.

We denote the space of metric-induced TPV configurations by

\[
\mathscr C_{\rm TPV}
\equiv
\Phi\!\left(\mathrm{Met}_{1,3}(M)\right).
\tag{21}
\]

There exists an inverse map

\[
g_{\mu\nu}
=
g_{\mu\nu}[\mathcal C].
\tag{22}
\]

This is the central formal result required for exact equivalence.

Importantly, \(\mathcal C\) is **not** an unrestricted 36-component constitutive tensor. Only constitutive configurations belonging to the metric-induced submanifold \(\mathscr C_{\rm TPV}\) are allowed. The apparent proliferation of constitutive components therefore introduces no new physical fields.

